      "final_message": "I rewrote `merge_intervals` in `solution.py` so it follows the README, and both public tests pass (`python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py`).\n\n- **Input checks:**\n  - If `intervals` isn't a list, it raises `TypeError`.\n  - It raises `ValueError` for any interval that isn't a two-element list or tuple, has non-int or bool values, or has `start > end`.\n- **Merging:** it sorts intervals by start and merges an interval into the previous one when it starts at or before that one's end, so `[1,2]` and `[2,3]` become `[1,3]`. Intervals that don't overlap or touch stay separate.\n- **Output:** it always returns a new list of `[start, end]` lists and never changes the input. An empty input returns `[]`.\n\nI also ran some ad-hoc checks beyond the public tests: touching intervals, tuple items, empty input, a tuple passed as `intervals`, and several invalid intervals. All behaved as the README says, and the input list was unchanged afterwards.\n\nDONE: Rewrote merge_intervals to validate input, sort by start and merge overlapping or touching intervals into a new list; public tests pass.",
